# Weather and light that read as weather, played through the real keys:
# a lamp that lays nothing on a bright land and a warm pool on a dark one, snow
# drawn pale against pale, a whiteout that takes the far field, dry lightning
# with its afterglow, and a wet night where the lights carry into the air.
#   tools/tour.sh tours/sky-polish.tour --seed=1
coast calm

# Noon on the snowfield, the brightest ground in the game. Press the real lamp
# key: the land must not change. (It used to lay a saturated pink disc here.)
place snowfield
hour 12
weather clear:0
wait 1.2
shot 01-snow-noon-drift-a
wait 0.25
shot 02-snow-noon-drift-b
tap lamp
wait 0.25
shot 03-snow-noon-lamp-lit with lamp
# Two frames of a running world are never identical: cloud shade walks, tufts
# sway, stipple crawls. So the first pair measures that drift over a quarter of
# a second with nothing done at all, and the second pair — the same quarter
# second with the lamp lit in the middle of it — must come in under WHAT THE
# FIRST PAIR MEASURED: the lamp adds no more to the page than standing still
# does. The crop is centred on the player, over the whole
# oval the disc used to cover (the playtest measured it about 470x330 frame
# pixels across, around the hand).
#
# THE CROP IS IN FRAME PIXELS AND A TOUR FRAME IS 3840x2160 — the base
# (UiBase.SIZE, 1920x1080) taken x2 by 98_tour._save_frame. It used to read
# 400,195,480,330, which is that oval written for the 1280x720 frames of the
# 640x360 floor and never rescaled when LANTERN moved the floor. On a 3840x2160
# frame that box is the TOP-LEFT CORNER — x 10..23%, y 9..24% of the picture.
# Looked at on this very staging it is BLANK SNOW: no player, no hand, no lamp,
# nothing that moves. So the two lines below, then the strictest pair of numbers
# in any tour (0.010 each), were measuring a patch of flat white a hundred feet
# from the lantern and passing for free — a lamp that laid a saturated pink disc over the
# whole snowfield would still have come in under the tolerance, because the disc
# would not have reached the corner being measured. x3 puts the box back on the
# fractions of the frame the old numbers meant: the same world oval, 6.9 units
# of ground round the player, at three times the pixels.
#
# Measured over the very same two pairs of frames, four runs, three machines:
#   drift, nothing done   old box 0.0005 / 0.0003   new box 0.0093 0.0094 0.0095 0.0097
#   with the lamp lit     old box 0.0006            new box 0.0074 0.0076 0.0078
# The old box was spending 6% of its allowance on flat snow, and the lamp moved
# it by three parts in ten thousand: thirty times LESS sensitive than no crop at
# all, on the one tour whose whole subject is a lamp that must lay nothing.
#
# AND THE SECOND LINE IS RELATIONAL NOW, which is what the paragraph above has
# always said in words. With both lines held to one absolute 0.010, the tight
# margin fell on the CONTROL pair — 0.0095 of 0.0100, 5% — and the control is a
# quarter second of a world with nothing done to it: snow, tufts and trees that
# sway on their own. So the tour was one noisy run from a red that would have
# said nothing whatever about the lamp, while the lamp itself sat at 0.0078 with
# 22% to spare. A number that measures how much the world animates is the wrong
# instrument for "the lamp changed nothing". `no_more_than` asks it directly, and
# the bar it sets now FOLLOWS the world: on a still minute the lamp is held to a
# far tighter number than 0.010 ever was, and on a windy one it is not failed for
# the wind.
#
# The 0.001 of slack is not a tolerance, it is the noise floor, and it is
# measured: across four runs on three machines the control pair came out 0.0093
# to 0.0097 and the lamp pair 0.0074 to 0.0078, so the run-to-run spread of
# either is about 0.0004. Slack matters because with the lamp laying nothing
# the two pairs are two samples of the SAME quarter-second of animation, and
# either may land above the other by chance; 0.001 is about two and a half times
# the spread, which keeps the effective bar at roughly the 0.010 it always was on
# a typical run while being much tighter on a quiet one. It is also two orders
# below what this test is hunting: the saturated disc it was written for covered
# most of this crop.
#
# THE FIRST LINE IS A SANITY CEILING, NOT THE LAMP'S BAR, and its number moved
# for that reason and no other. It was 0.010, which is what BOTH lines were held
# to while the lamp had no bar of its own — and with the crop finally on the
# player it measured 0.0093 to 0.0097 across four runs on three machines. Three
# percent from failing, on snow and tufts swaying with nothing done to them.
# A guard that fires on the world breathing is not a guard: every red it threw
# would have been meaningless, and meaningless reds are how a file stops being
# believed and gets waved through with the one real red inside it.
# So 0.03, which is three times the measured drift. It is not room bought for the
# lamp — the lamp is held below, relationally, and tighter than 0.010 ever was on
# a still minute. It is the question "was this world quiet enough for the
# comparison under it to mean anything", and 0.03 is the first number that can
# only be answered NO by something genuinely flickering.
# IF IT GOES RED, IT IS NOT THE LANTERN. Look at what is moving in the crop —
# snow falling, tufts swaying, stipple crawling, a shader that made one of them
# livelier — and either explain the rise or fix it, because at 0.03 a red here
# means this world has started animating three times as hard as it did the day
# the number was measured.
same 01-snow-noon-drift-a 02-snow-noon-drift-b 0.03 1200,585,1440,990
same 02-snow-noon-drift-b 03-snow-noon-lamp-lit no_more_than 01-snow-noon-drift-a 02-snow-noon-drift-b 0.001 1200,585,1440,990
tap lamp
wait 0.4

# The same ground at night: now the lamp is the light, and the pool it lays is
# its own warm, in flat steps, over cold snow.
hour 23
wait 0.8
shot 04-snow-night-lamp-out with unlit
tap lamp
wait 0.6
shot 05-snow-night-lamp-lit with lamp
tap lamp
wait 0.4

# Snow falling on lying snow: paper flecks held by a shade, never dark specks.
hour 13
weather snow:1
wait 1.4
shot 06-snowfield-snow-pale-flecks with land:snowfield
# A whiteout: the far field goes in blowing white, the near field still reads.
weather whiteout:1
wait 1.4
shot 07-snowfield-whiteout with land:snowfield

# The bonelands at dusk: dry lightning with no rain in it. Wait for a real
# strike, then catch the light rolling on through the cloud.
place bonelands
hour 20.2
weather dry_storm:1
wait 1.0
shot 08-bonelands-dry-storm with land:bonelands
await strike 60
wait 0.35
shot 09-bonelands-afterglow-rolls with land:bonelands
# The drawn bolt lives four frames: too quick to be sure of catching by waiting
# for one, and a tour that only sometimes has a bolt in its picture proves
# nothing. So the still that must show one asks the sky to hold it — the same
# held moment a shot gets (10_sky HELD_STRIKE), the cloud at its brightest with
# the machines' power back up between two of its dips.
weather dry_storm:1:bolt
wait 0.5
shot 10-bonelands-bolt-held with land:bonelands
# Hard white glare on the same stone at noon.
hour 12.5
weather glare:1
wait 1.2
shot 11-bonelands-glare with land:bonelands

# The burning's furnace haze, lying low and warm at dusk.
place burning
hour 19.4
weather haze:1
wait 1.4
shot 12-burning-furnace-haze with land:burning

# A wet night in the first village: every pool the colour of the light that
# made it, the stolen neon spilling into the rain, and the lantern walked
# through it. Then the same village at noon, where nothing spills at all.
village 0
hour 22.5
weather rain:0.8
wait 1.6
shot 13-village-wet-night
tap lamp
wait 0.6
shot 14-village-wet-lantern with lamp
walk 1,0 1.2
shot 15-walked-with-the-lantern with lamp
tap lamp
hour 12
wait 1.2
shot 16-village-wet-noon

# The village square at 23:00, close in: the frame a pool must not flatten. The
# fire's logs against its flames, the bench, the cobbles and the player's own
# coat, trousers and boots all still read inside the lantern's own light.
hour 23
weather clear:0
zoom 9
wait 1.2
shot 17-village-night-pool-out with unlit
tap lamp
wait 0.8
shot 18-village-night-pool-lit with lamp
tap lamp
weather rules
